---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/recart-alternative';
const title = "Recart alternatives: transferring a managed SMS program";
const desc = "Replace the work as well as the sender: capture promises, cart queues, number continuity and customer replies need named owners.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should a store keep Recart?",
    "answer": "When its capture, SMS execution and managed work meet the brief and economics. Repair a specific calendar or data defect before assuming replacement is required."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will the number and queued messages transfer automatically?",
    "answer": "Confirm number release and registration with both providers. Choose explicitly how old queues finish or cancel; do not replay them from an audience import."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can an email tool replace the whole program?",
    "answer": "An email tool can address an email brief. SMS delivery, capture promises, replies and remaining cart work still need verified owners."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Departure planning · November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Decide which responsibility is changing</h2>
    <p>Recart is not just a cart-recovery tool. Its <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/recart">Shopify listing</a> describes capture, campaigns and automations, while its <a class="link" href="https://recart.com/fully-managed-sms">managed service offer</a> includes program assistance. Keeping that combination can be sensible when the merchant needs an operating partner. A calendar problem may call for a revised brief rather than replacement. First list the work the manager currently does and who would do it after departure.</p>
    <p>Separate three decisions: replace the SMS sender, replace capture, or bring campaign operations in house. Moving email education alone does not require moving the number. Read the <a class="link" href="/apps/recart">Recart operating review</a> for initial evaluation; this guide concerns an already-running program and its unfinished obligations.</p>
    <h2>Make a departure register before changing capture</h2>
    <p>Record every live popup, keyword, landing page and printed link, its destination, promised benefit and disclosure version. Export permitted contact data together with available source, timestamp and subscription evidence; keep exclusions distinct from eligible subscribers. Ask what export fields are available rather than assuming a CSV recreates the whole program. Reconcile totals and investigate duplicates and missing records before importing.</p>
    <p>Proposed acceptance fixture: a returning mobile visitor submits the old capture form while the replacement is being enabled. Inspect which system accepted the number, which benefit was issued and which welcome is queued. Repeat with a changed number, a declined email choice and an interrupted submission. Require one accountable capture owner per placement; do not infer transferable recognition from an old successful signup.</p>
    <h2>Keep the number and reply desk reachable</h2>
    <p>Request written confirmation from both providers on sender ownership, release requirements, registration, destinations and the point at which traffic changes. Do not cancel a working number on a presumed port date. Inventory support integrations and open conversations separately from campaigns. Assign an owner for unanswered product questions, complaints, STOP and HELP handling throughout the transition.</p>
    <p>Proposed fixture: send a controlled question before the change, reply after it, then inspect the old and new inboxes and the customer record. Check an opt-out arriving through the old path after the export. Establish how that change reaches any remaining eligible audience before dispatch. Historic Messenger references do not prove present sending access; obtain account-specific confirmation if it remains part of the program.</p>
    <h2>Retire cart queues without replaying them</h2>
    <p>For each active cart or browse journey, record the event identity, delay, offer, remaining steps and current sender. Choose whether the old system finishes eligible work or cancels it before the replacement takes new entrants. Importing an audience must not silently restart welcome or recovery from step one. Keep a checkpoint of queue state and a way to inspect changes since that checkpoint.</p>
    <p>Proposed fixtures: purchase before dispatch, purchase on another device, duplicate cart event, sold-out variant and opt-out after entry. Inspect the actual queued action and its cancellation reason. During rollback, pause the replacement and reconcile already-sent work before restoring the old path. A successful connection is not evidence that either provider cancels the other's queue.</p>
    <h2>Use real campaign needs to shortlist replacements</h2>
    <p>Ask <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> to demonstrate the required SMS workflow and service scope. Ask <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> to demonstrate a shared email/SMS operating model if that is the goal. These are candidates, not a ranked result. Consider <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> for an email brief only after confirming the needed integration; an email move leaves SMS ownership to resolve.</p>
    <p>Use your next release as a proposed exercise: VIP access without a discount, variant-specific waitlist priority, a gift bundle versus self-purchase education, and subscriber exclusion from an unsuitable flash offer. Provide real inventory and audience rules. Require a preview of eligibility and a controlled queue inspection; no vendor should receive credit for an unperformed outcome. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/segment-by-value">value segments</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a>.</p>
    <h2>Budget for the operator who replaces the manager</h2>
    <p>Obtain current quotes for software, usage, carrier charges, managed work, setup, overlap and number-related work. Include internal copy review, replies and incident cover. Model both a quiet month with fixed commitments and a peak release with message segments, not only contact count. See the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a>; no fixed price or universal migration duration is assumed here.</p>
    <p>Illustrative order economics: $100 before a $10 discount gives $90 collected; subtract $45 product and fulfillment cost to leave $45 before channel and service costs. Do not subtract that discount again. Compare contribution per assigned eligible customer with a stable holdout over a suitable observation window, then subtract fixed and transition costs separately. Attributed recovery revenue alone does not measure the gain from switching.</p>
    <h2>Release only when the remaining work has an owner</h2>
    <p>Name the person who approves copy, validates inventory, pauses sends and answers replies. Keep capture evidence, channel-specific opt-outs, recipient-time sending restrictions and purchase exits inspectable. A decline in one channel must not be overwritten by an import from another. Set merchant-specific contact limits and stop conditions using the <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign calendar</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours</a> guides.</p>
    <p>Accept the handoff when the placement register, number path, reply desk, exclusions and remaining queue reconcile with observed controlled tests. Record unresolved exceptions and keep affected paths paused. Retain exports and the decision log securely under your retention policy; do not erase the old program's evidence simply because the new dashboard is populated.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
